{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-275499",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "For a continuous random variable $X$ and a function $r(x),$ the rule of the lazy statistician states that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X)]={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn this problem, the break point $X$ is uniformly distributed over the interval $[0,1]$, so the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x)=1,\\,0\\le x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nWhen the stick is broken at point $X$, it is divided into two pieces: one of length $X$ and the other of length $1-X.$ The longer of the two pieces, $Y,$ is therefore given by\n\n$$\nY=max(X,1-X).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we define\n\n$$\nr(x)=max(x,1-x).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the integral, we express $r(x)$ as an explicit piecewise function. Observe that,\n\n- if $0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}$, then $x\\le 1-x$, so the longer piece has length $1-x,$\n\n- if $\\frac{1}{2}<x\\le 1$, then $x>1-x$, so the longer piece has length $x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nr(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-x, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ x, & \\,\\frac{1}{2}<x\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now apply the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & =\\text{E}[max(X,1-X)] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }r(x)f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(1-x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(1-x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1/2}+{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8})+(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}+\\frac{3}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is $\\frac{3}{4}.$",
